In the one-room, Celty’s phone buzzed against her glove.
Izaya was still watching Mikado’s screen like it was a birth.
Mikado was still typing a net into existence with shaking hands.
Celty angled the glass against her coat, helmet tipping just enough that the light would not carry.
The private line opened under the text, quieter than keys.
You already showed me the bike. You already said you were the same person. Do not pretend this is a new secret between us.
Then she typed, because Izaya’s eyes flicked once toward the motion of her hands.
Celty: I know.
Celty: Do it.
Celty: Face stays off this hunt.
Celty: Tech Rider can move where Curtis Nightstar would start another rumor.
Celty: I will keep Orihara on the boy and the color.
Celty: You keep the streets.
Celty: Thank you for not leaving bodies behind Mikado’s building.
A thinner line, sent after a second of thumb-hover.
Celty: Come back when the mask is on.
Celty: Not to this room.
Celty: To me.
She locked the phone before Izaya could invent a reason to ask.
“Work,” she typed on the PDA, turning it out for the room.
Courier.
Izaya smiled without looking away from the Dollars homepage.
“Everyone’s so busy tonight. The foreigner takes coats for a walk. The rider takes a private text. The first-year builds a country. I almost feel left out.”
Mikado did not look up.
“You’re not left out. You’re just not invited.”
Celty’s helmet stayed on Mikado.
In her pocket, the agreement was already moving toward a closet and a white helmet and a name the city had coined without permission.
On a broken one-room floor,
Celty Sturluson hid a thread
Izaya would have sold a district to read.
Curtis Nightstar chose the mask
the street had already named.
The men were leaving Japan.
The face was leaving the hunt.
And the Tech Rider
was coming back
undead.